  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front remains draped across the forecast area this\nafternoon. Along and north of the boundary some very light rain\ncontinues. I do not anticipate much, generally less than a tenth\nof an inch, but the dampness and cloud cover will help to\nmitigate any new wildfire threat and allow crews to catch up\nwith any ongoing fires. \n\nAs the front continues to drift north into Wed some very warm\nair is forecast locally. Model guidance is pushing the 925 mb\ntemp forecast into the +16 or +17C range. This is good enough\nfor high temps at least into the mid 70s. This is at least 20\ndegrees above normal and will be pushing record values, which\ncan be found below in the Climate section.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBeginning Wed the upper low over the Midwest will begin to open\nup and shear out to the northeast. This will tend to leave\nbehind the frontal boundary that will eventually slow to a crawl\nas it nears the East Coast. At the same time Thu into Fri a\nnorthern stream shortwave is forecast to drop into the Great\nLakes and help close off another upper low. This will help keep\nthe frontal boundary locked in place and help low pressure to\ndevelop along that front. This is forecast to zip north and\nbring a pretty solid rainfall event to areas in the vicinity.\nThe maine question will be where this front stalls. As it stands\nright now that looks pretty close to our coastline. The chances\nfor rain over the eastern half of the forecast area are pretty\nhigh, and probabilities over at least an inch of QPF are also\nfairly high. ECMWF EPS guidance continues to trend wetter, as\ndoes the GEFS even though it is slightly drier than its European\ncounterpart. NBM PoP forecast looks pretty good for this range,\nespecially since there is some timing uncertainty even if it\ndoes look like rain along the coast.",
